# The three Table 5 cycle types no other fixture reaches.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. This fixture exists for one reason: phase 6
# of docs/PLAN.md exits on "every Table 5 cycle type has a vector", and an audit
# of the .expected files found three rows that no decode had ever run over.
#
# They were invisible because the *table* tests walk all 18 rows and the
# mutation suite kills a mutation on any of them -- both check the row against
# the transcription, and neither needs the decoder to have decoded one. A row
# can be perfectly transcribed, perfectly mutation-covered, and still have a
# header layout that has never been used to read a packet.
#
# --- 1. Memory Write 64: the 11-byte header, carrying data ---
#
# put_np_memory_read.espi already reaches the 11-byte header on a Memory Read 64,
# which has no payload. This is the same header with data behind it, so the
# payload offset after an 8-byte address is exercised.
#
#   00     PUT_PC
#   03     Cycle Type: Memory Write 64        Table 5, p.47
#          Odd, so it carries data -- section 4.1.1's LSB rule, p.46.
#   10     Tag 1, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   02     Length[7:0] = 02h -> 2 bytes
#   00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00    Address, most significant byte first
#
#          Section 4.1.4, p.51: "When 64 bits addressing format is used, the
#          upper 32 bits address [63:32] must not be all 0". So the address is
#          0000_0001_0000_0000h, which is legal; an address below 4 GB here
#          would be a warning, and that path is already covered by
#          put_np_memory_read.espi.
#
#   DE AD  Data
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 00 03 10 02 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 DE AD B7
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95

# --- 2. Message with Data: a message code Table 6 does not name ---
#
# put_pc_ltr_message.espi covers the Message row and Table 6's one code. This is
# the other message row -- the one that carries a payload after the four message
# specific bytes -- and it deliberately uses a code Table 6 has never heard of.
#
#   00     PUT_PC
#   11     Cycle Type: 0001 r2r1r0 1 with r2r1r0 = 000b
#          Message with Data, routing "Local -- terminated at receiver"
#   20     Tag 2, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   03     Length[7:0] = 03h -> 3 bytes
#
#          Note the contrast with the plain Message row, whose Length is
#          Reserved and must be all 0s (p.55). Same figure, same header, and the
#          field means different things on the two rows.
#
#   02     Message Code -- not 01h, so not LTR, and Table 6 defines nothing else
#   AA BB CC DD   the four message specific bytes
#
#          With no transcribed layout behind the code, these are reported as
#          four numbered bytes rather than resolved. That is the honest answer,
#          and it is a different answer from LTR's fields being decoded.
#
#   11 22 33      Data
CMD 00 11 20 03 02 AA BB CC DD 11 22 33 4D
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95

# --- 3. Unsuccessful Completion Without Data, on the FLASH channel ---
#
# completion_split.espi walks the whole P1P0 field on the peripheral channel.
# Table 5 prints this row twice, once per channel, and stage E gave the flash
# copy its own header layout -- so note 2's P1 rule now applies to two rows
# rather than one. Nothing had ever decoded the flash copy.
#
#   0B     GET_FLASH_C
#   31     CRC
#
#   08     ACCEPT
#   0E     Cycle Type: 00001 P1P0 0 with P1P0 = 11b
#          Unsuccessful Completion Without Data, "only completion for a split
#          transaction".
#
#          Note 2, p.49: "For Unsuccessful Completion without Data, P1 must be
#          always a '1' as this is always the last or the only completion." 11b
#          satisfies it. The forbidden encodings 00b and 01b are exercised on
#          the peripheral copy in completion_split.espi.
#
#   40     Tag 4, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   00     Length[7:0] = 00h, driven to zero as section 4.1.3 requires
#   0F 01  Status
#   42     CRC
CMD 0B 31
TAR
RSP 08 0E 40 00 0F 01 42
